    "result_text": "The full suite passes and the code compiles. There's only one test (`AcceptanceTest`) plus non-`@Test` base classes.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\n**What I found:** The existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice` already renders `ProblemDetail` responses for Bean-Validation failures (`MethodArgumentNotValidException`), 500s, and data-integrity errors. But `OwnerRestControllerV1` produced its business-rule rejections as **bodiless** `ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST/CONFLICT/TOO_MANY_REQUESTS)` \u2014 no problem+json body at all. These were the only manual client-error rejections in the codebase (V2 and other controllers have none).\n\n**Change (kept small and single-responsibility):**\n\n1. **New `RejectedRequestException`** (`rest/advice/`) \u2014 carries the intended `HttpStatus` plus a human-readable detail. One job: signal a business-rule rejection that should render as a problem.\n\n2. **New handler in `ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 `handleRejectedRequestException` maps the exception's status and message through the *existing* `detailBuild(...)` helper, so RFC7807 formatting stays in exactly one place (reused, not copied). `detailBuild` already sets all four required members (`type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`), and `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` yields the `application/problem+json` content type.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1`** \u2014 replaced the eight bodiless rejections (5 in `addOwner`, 3 in `updateOwner`) with `throw new RejectedRequestException(status, detail)`, each with a meaningful message. HTTP status codes are unchanged; the controller no longer carries any response-formatting concern.\n\nThe controller keeps its request-handling logic while all error-body construction lives in the advice.",
